+Manual recovery playbook — Evidence pipeline (cosign / Rekor) failures
+
+This document contains step-by-step guidance if the evidence pipeline cannot cosign, upload to S3, or register with Rekor.
+
+1) Diagnose
+ - Check the uploader pending directory (default /tmp/evidence_pending).
+   - ls -la /tmp/evidence_pending
+ - Inspect uploader logs (CI job output or ops/evidence/uploader.py logs).
+ - Check network access to EVIDENCE_BUCKET, cosign KMS, and REKOR_SERVER.
+
+2) Attempt automated reupload
+ - Run the CI reupload script that the release gate uses:
+   - bash ops/verify/ci_steps.sh
+ - This runs python3 ops/evidence/uploader.py and then checks pending directory; report results in CI.
+
+3) Manual reupload steps
+ - For each manifest in /tmp/evidence_pending:
+   - Try cosign signing locally (if COSIGN_KMS_URI available):
+     cosign sign-blob --key $COSIGN_KMS_URI /tmp/evidence_pending/manifest.json
+   - Upload to S3:
+     aws s3 cp /tmp/evidence_pending/manifest.json s3://$EVIDENCE_BUCKET/evidence/
+   - Register with Rekor (if rekor-cli configured):
+     rekor-cli upload --artifact /tmp/evidence_pending/manifest.json
+ - If cosign KMS is unavailable, sign with a recovery key only after authorization from security lead; record reasons in an incident ticket.
+
+4) Recovery & reconciliation
+ - After successful uploads/registration, remove the file from pending directory.
+ - Update the evidence_index.json (ops/verify/generate_rekor_index.py) to include the new Rekor entries.
+ - Re-run CI release gate or mark the release as retried.
+
+5) Post-incident
+ - Create a remediation issue with the recovered artifacts and the recovery procedures performed.
+ - If cosign KMS outage is due to key compromise, rotate keys immediately and update COSIGN_KMS_URI.
+ - Run the key_usage_monitor (ops/security/key_usage_monitor.py) to gather key usage evidence.
+
+Contact: security@your-org (replace with your security contact).
+
